= Intrigues in the Imperial Court

Word reaches the capital of an address given at the winter court of <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Axton**</span>. Empress Jacqueline Scilos, three years crowned and no longer treated by her ministers as a child in her father's chair, has told the assembled houses what she intends her reign to be for: the reunification and pacification of the continent, pursued through every avenue available to her — diplomatic, dynastic, commercial, or military.

She named no realm. She did not have to. The Commonwealth was the Empire's second province within living memory, and the court has never once used the past tense about it.

The dispatches disagree about how seriously to take her. Some read a young sovereign staking out a position before a court that doubts her. Others note that she said *military* out loud, to an audience that includes the men who would have to do it, and that nobody in the hall seemed surprised.

Whatever she meant by it, we are asked for our position.

- @republican
- @concerning
- @imperial_origins

@republican
title: The Marchers remain a fundamentally republican party.
subtitle: Say plainly that the Commonwealth's independence is not a matter for negotiation.
on-arrival: empire_posture -= 4 if empire_posture >= 4; center_dissent -= 3; left_dissent -= 3

We answer without hedging: the Commonwealth was made by its own people and will not be unmade by a foreign court, whatever the lady in Axton tells her houses. It is the oldest position our party holds and the one our membership never has to be persuaded of — the centrists and the left close ranks behind it gladly.

The Imperial legation registers the statement. Nothing further is said, and something in the correspondence cools by a degree.

@concerning
title: This is a concerning development.
subtitle: Note it, and commit to nothing.

We call the address concerning, urge the government to watch the situation closely, and decline to say anything that could be quoted back at us later. It is the safest thing we could have published and it commits us to precisely nothing.

@imperial_origins
title: The Marchers began in the Empire...
subtitle: Acknowledge the shared history. Our own left will not thank us for it.
on-arrival: empire_posture += 8 if empire_posture <= 92; center_dissent += 8; left_dissent += 8

We say the thing that is true and unwelcome: the Marchers were founded in the Empire, by Imperial workers, in Imperial cities, decades before there was a Commonwealth to be loyal to. Our oldest lodges still keep their charters from Axton. If the Empress speaks of a continent that ought not to be at war with itself, she is not speaking a language foreign to us.

The Imperial court receives this warmly — a reformist party in the lost province that does not treat the word *reunion* as an obscenity is worth cultivating.

Our own party receives it rather differently. The centrists think it reckless and the left thinks it something worse; both spend the following weeks saying so at length, and in print.
